The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{x+1}{x^2+1}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{x+1}{x^{2}}}{\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^{2}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{\cancel{x}}{x\cdot\cancel{x}}+\dfrac{1}{x^2}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{1}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{x}+\dfrac{1}{x^2}}{1+\dfrac{1}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0+0}{1+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{1}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$
